# Mutation cases for "the Kotlin gate's engine/language array is the routes
# this backend actually has" — the JVM half of that claim.
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/kotlin_engine_pairs_jvm.cases`.
#
# ## Why this file exists beside `kotlin_engine_selection.cases`
#
# The population claim `scripts/gates/w3c-kotlin.sh` makes with
# `KOTLIN_ENGINE_PAIRS` is answered in two places that do not imply each
# other. The GENERATOR side — which languages `sce-codegen` can emit a Kotlin
# artifact for — is Rust, and `kotlin_engine_selection.cases` reaches it
# through a cargo round. The ENGINE side — which languages each engine says it
# accepts — is Kotlin, and until the Gradle runner existed nothing in the
# corpus could reach it at all. Its red witness was bought by hand on
# 2026-08-30 (delete the `lua:lua` row, run the suite, read `expected 4 pairs,
# was 3`, put it back), and a hand-bought red is evidence about the day it was
# bought: `--check` never asks whether it still applies, and the weekly sweep
# cannot re-prove it.
#
# So this is the first casefile in the corpus driven by `mutation_gradle`, and
# what it studies is `GateEnginePairsTest` — the test that derives the
# population from `W3CTestBase.KNOWN_ENGINES` × `ScxmlScriptEngine
# .acceptsLanguage` and compares it with the array the gate declares.
#
# ⚠ The direction that matters is the MISSING row. A row naming a pair
# nothing supports fails loudly the moment the gate runs it — the engine
# refuses the language, in its own words. A pair the array OMITS fails
# nowhere: the gate spends its minutes on the rows it has and reports success.
# Case 1 is that direction, and it is the reason the test was written.

# The suite is selected by task path and narrowed by `--tests`, which is
# scoped to the task named beside it. `:sce-kotlin-tests:test` unfiltered is
# 373 conformance cases per run and this round rebuilds between every case;
# the filter is what makes a JVM round affordable rather than daily.
mutation_gradle . :sce-kotlin-tests:test --tests 'com.sce.w3c.GateEnginePairsTest'

# Named explicitly, because only a cargo selector carries its oracle inside
# itself (`--test <name>`). Without this the round would report verdicts that
# nothing re-proves when the assertions behind them are weakened.
mutation_oracles backends/kotlin/tests/src/test/kotlin/com/sce/w3c/GateEnginePairsTest.kt

# The gate is READ AT RUN TIME — `GateEnginePairsTest` opens the file and
# parses the array out of it — so it is a runtime target: no compiled artifact
# changes when it is edited, and demanding a changed one would call every case
# here INCONCLUSIVE.
mutation_runtime_targets scripts/gates/w3c-kotlin.sh

# The engine side is COMPILED INTO the suite through the module graph, so
# these are ordinary targets and the round can hash what the edit produced.
mutation_targets backends/kotlin/runtime/src/commonMain/kotlin/com/sce/runtime/ScxmlScriptEngine.kt
mutation_targets backends/kotlin/lua/src/main/kotlin/com/sce/scripting/lua/LuaScriptEngine.kt

# ── The gate stops naming a route the backend has ──────────────────
#
# `lua:lua` is the row that took a year to arrive: the build-time lowering
# path, an engine handed an artifact generated for Lua. Deleting it leaves the
# gate green — it runs three pairs and passes all three — and the route goes
# unmeasured. Equality, not containment, is what turns that red.
mutation_case "the gate drops the engine/language pair that measures lowering" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/gates/w3c-kotlin.sh",
     "KOTLIN_ENGINE_PAIRS=(rhino:ecmascript quickjs:ecmascript lua:ecmascript lua:lua)",
     "KOTLIN_ENGINE_PAIRS=(rhino:ecmascript quickjs:ecmascript lua:ecmascript)")
PY

# ── The engine stops claiming the language it adapts ───────────────
#
# The other side of the same equality. `LuaScriptEngine` accepts both
# languages because it owns the seam that lowers ECMAScript; narrowing it to
# the native language alone leaves the gate declaring `lua:ecmascript`, a row
# no engine answers. The population derivation must notice, or the array is
# being compared against a list this test keeps rather than against the code.
mutation_case "the Lua engine stops accepting the language it has an adapter for" <<'PY'
edit("backends/kotlin/lua/src/main/kotlin/com/sce/scripting/lua/LuaScriptEngine.kt",
     "override fun acceptsLanguage(language: ScriptLanguage): Boolean = true",
     "override fun acceptsLanguage(language: ScriptLanguage): Boolean =\n"
     "        language == nativeLanguage()")
PY

# ── Every engine claims every language ─────────────────────────────
#
# The default `acceptsLanguage` is what makes an ECMAScript engine refuse Lua
# rather than fail inside somebody else's syntax. Widened to `true`, Rhino and
# QuickJS both claim a Lua route neither has, and the derived population grows
# past the array. This is the direction the gate would otherwise catch by
# running the row and watching the engine refuse — the point of the case is
# that the DERIVATION catches it first, without spending the minutes.
mutation_case "the default engine contract accepts every language" <<'PY'
edit("backends/kotlin/runtime/src/commonMain/kotlin/com/sce/runtime/ScxmlScriptEngine.kt",
     "fun acceptsLanguage(language: ScriptLanguage): Boolean = language == nativeLanguage()",
     "fun acceptsLanguage(language: ScriptLanguage): Boolean = true")
PY
